          "tool_use_id": "toolu_017oQ9rAhhBhahgSsd5PaVDi",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/consistent-hashing.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Consistent Hashing\\ndescription: A technique that maps cache keys and nodes onto a circular keyspace to minimize reshuffling when nodes are added or removed.\\ntimestamp: 2026-08-17T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/ConsistentHashing\\n  - pattern/Distributed-Systems\\n  - tech/Caching\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single clear source explaining the core mechanism and virtual nodes optimization.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nConsistent hashing maps both cache keys and cache nodes onto the same circular keyspace. When a node is added to or removed from the system, only the keys that fall between the new/removed node and its neighbour are remapped, rather than reshuffling the entire keyspace. This property makes consistent hashing particularly valuable for distributed caching systems.\\n\\n## Virtual Nodes\\n\\nIn a small cluster, the physical distribution of nodes around the hash ring can create significant load imbalance. Virtual nodes address this by assigning each physical node multiple positions on the ring. This even distribution reduces the variance in how keys are distributed across the cache, improving throughput and cache hit rates.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source: eval://log-no-day-grouping/sample-10\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
